# Registry block media (images & video)

Use this when a block needs raster images (PNG, JPG, WebP) or video (MP4, WebM). Most blocks (navbars, SVG-only sections, etc.) do not need this.

## How it works

Every block resolves media **local-first with a baked-in CDN fallback** via
[`lib/block-media.ts`](../../lib/block-media.ts). The entry file (or, for
client-interactive blocks, a thin Server Component wrapper — see below)
checks the consumer's own filesystem at render time. If they've dropped a real
asset for that slot, it wins — no code edit required. Otherwise it falls back
to the CDN so a fresh install still renders working images:

```tsx
import { createBlockImage } from "@/lib/block-media"

const blockImage = createBlockImage("gallery-section-v3")
const src = blockImage("image-1.jpg") // also finds image-1.png, .webp, etc.
```

- Fresh install, no local asset yet → CDN URL, image renders immediately.
- Consumer drops a file with the **same base name** and any supported extension
  (e.g. `image.jpeg` when the block references `image.png`) → local file wins.
- Exact canonical filename always wins when present (overwrite-in-place).
- This requires whatever calls `blockImage`/`blockVideo` to run on the
  **server** (Node.js runtime, not Edge) — `node:fs` cannot run in a browser
  bundle.

**If the block's UI is client-interactive** (mobile menu state, GSAP/Motion
animation, simulated page-load, etc.), the media constant cannot live in that
`"use client"` file — `node:fs` in a client bundle fails at build/dev time
with "the chunking context does not support external modules". Split it:

- Keep the block's public entry file (the one `page.tsx` imports, still a
  zero-prop `<BlockName />`) as a **Server Component** — no `"use client"`.
  It computes the URL via `blockImage`/`blockVideo` and renders the client
  piece, passing the resolved URL down as a prop (e.g. `logoSrc`, `bgSrc`,
  `screenSrc`).
- Move the interactive JSX into a sibling `<block-id>-root.tsx` (`"use
  client"`), which accepts that URL as a prop instead of computing it.
- If the media is used inside a further-nested helper component (e.g. a
  `BrandLogo` rendered from inside the root), thread the prop through — don't
  reach for a module-level constant from a nested scope.
- Register the new `-root.tsx` file in `registry.json` alongside the
  existing entry (`registry:component`, same `target` convention).

This is exactly the `hero-section-v6.tsx` / `hero-v6-root.tsx` and
`navbar-section-v1.tsx` / `navbar-section-v1-root.tsx` split — look at either
for a concrete example.

There used to be a build-time-rewrite alternative (same-origin path in
source, rewritten to a hardcoded CDN URL in the shipped JSON after
`shadcn build`). It was removed: once rewritten, the shipped source never
reads the consumer's `public/` folder again, so replacing a same-named local
asset did nothing — the consumer had to hand-edit the installed file to point
back to a local path. That defeated the actual product promise (swap the
asset, it just works). Every block in this repo is on the local-first
pattern now; don't reintroduce the rewrite approach.

## Consumer install

```bash
npx shadcn@latest add "https://uiception.com/r/<block-id>.json"
```

That's it. CDN images render immediately, zero setup.

**To swap an asset:** drop a file with the same base name into the
pre-created `public/images/blocks/<block-id>/` folder (created by the
`.gitkeep` files included in the registry item) and reload. Supported image
formats: jpg, jpeg, png, webp, avif, gif, svg. Supported video formats: mp4,
webm, mov, m4v. No code edit required.

## Authoring: adding media to a block

### 1. Use `createBlockImage` / `createBlockVideo` from `@/lib/block-media`

Add `https://uiception.com/r/block-media.json` to the block's
`registryDependencies`. In the entry file:

```tsx
import { createBlockImage, createBlockVideo } from "@/lib/block-media"

const blockImage = createBlockImage("<block-id>")
const blockVideo = createBlockVideo("<block-id>")
```

**`next/image`** — always add `unoptimized` so consumers don't need `remotePatterns` in their own `next.config` for the CDN fallback:

```tsx
<Image src={HERO_BG} unoptimized alt="" fill />
```

> **Why `unoptimized` is required (do not remove):** Without it, `next/image` rewrites `src` to `/_next/image?url=...`, which hits the consumer's own image optimization API. Without `remotePatterns` for `uiception.com` in their config that request returns a 400 — broken image, nothing shown. `unoptimized` bypasses that entirely and fetches directly from the CDN, zero config required. The tradeoff is that previews on uiception.com itself don't benefit from Next.js image optimization (WebP conversion, resizing, caching) — but consumer experience is the priority.

**Plain string constant** (shader props, video `src`):

```tsx
const VIDEO_SRC = blockVideo("screen-demo.mp4")
```

### 2. Keep media URLs in the entry file — not in `config.ts`

`config.ts` holds pure data (types, labels, item arrays). URL construction belongs in the entry file.

```ts
// config.ts — pure data only
export const imageFiles = [
  { file: "photo-1.jpg", alt: "Description" },
] as const
```

```tsx
// entry file — URL construction here
const items = imageFiles.map(({ file, alt }) => ({
  src: blockImage(file),
  alt,
}))
```

### 3. Add a `.gitkeep` per asset in `registry.json`

Include one empty `.gitkeep` file per expected asset so the consumer's `public/` folder is pre-created on install. They see exactly which files to replace.

```json
{
  "path": "public/images/blocks/<block-id>/hero-bg.png.gitkeep",
  "type": "registry:file",
  "target": "public/images/blocks/<block-id>/hero-bg.png.gitkeep"
}
```

Create the matching empty file in this repo under `public/images/blocks/<block-id>/`.

### 4. No binary files, no env vars, no extra dependencies

### 5. Place assets in `public/`

```
public/images/blocks/<block-id>/   ← images + .gitkeep files
public/videos/blocks/<block-id>/   ← videos + .gitkeep files
```

## What NOT to do

| ❌ Don't | ✅ Do |
|---|---|
| `mediaOrigin` / env vars in block source | `createBlockImage` / `createBlockVideo` local-first + CDN fallback |
| Build-time rewrite to a hardcoded CDN-only URL | Local-first check so a swapped asset needs no code edit |
| Inline `existsSync` helpers in block source | Import from `@/lib/block-media` (registry dependency) |
| `"use client"` on the file that calls `blockImage` | Keep it a Server Component; only leaf UI needs client |
| Module-level media constant read from a nested client helper | Thread the resolved URL down as a prop instead |
| Single `.gitkeep` for the whole folder | One `<asset>.gitkeep` per expected file |
| Put URLs in `config.ts` | Filenames in config; URL construction in entry file |
| `next/image` without `unoptimized` on media src | Add `unoptimized` — consumers need no `remotePatterns` |
| List binary files in `registry.json` | Only `.tsx`, `.ts`, `.css`, `.gitkeep` source files |
